1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,950
Welcome to Module Two.

2
00:00:01,950 --> 00:00:03,725
Think about mutation rate.

3
00:00:03,725 --> 00:00:07,112
Too low, and a lineage is stable but stuck.

4
00:00:07,112 --> 00:00:13,175
Too high, and it explores wildly, but noise piles up and good solutions get lost.

5
00:00:13,175 --> 00:00:17,912
Somewhere in between is a useful balance, and the right balance depends on the job.

6
00:00:17,912 --> 00:00:22,587
A language model faces that same trade-off at every single word it writes.

7
00:00:22,587 --> 00:00:26,025
Yesterday was about training, how the weights are shaped.

8
00:00:26,025 --> 00:00:30,462
Today is about what happens when a trained model actually produces text.

9
00:00:30,462 --> 00:00:32,962
First, a small piece of vocabulary.

10
00:00:32,962 --> 00:00:35,837
Models do not read words or letters.

11
00:00:35,837 --> 00:00:40,575
They read tokens, which are chunks of text, often pieces of words.

12
00:00:40,575 --> 00:00:47,125
Tokens are also how cost, usage limits and memory are counted, so you will hear that word a lot in this course.

13
00:00:47,125 --> 00:00:48,712
Now the key idea.

14
00:00:48,712 --> 00:00:51,875
At each step, the model does not pick a word.

15
00:00:51,875 --> 00:00:57,712
It scores every possible next token, and turns those scores into a probability distribution.

16
00:00:57,712 --> 00:01:07,937
For a sentence like, perform the concerto for, blank, a good model spreads its bets over violin, piano and trumpet, and puts almost nothing on airplane.

17
00:01:07,937 --> 00:01:12,950
Then a separate piece of machinery, the sampler, draws one token from that distribution.

18
00:01:12,950 --> 00:01:16,750
The token is added to the text, and the whole thing repeats.

19
00:01:16,750 --> 00:01:19,512
Temperature is the dial on that sampler.

20
00:01:19,512 --> 00:01:24,950
Low temperature sharpens the distribution, so the top choice almost always wins.

21
00:01:24,950 --> 00:01:28,862
High temperature flattens it, so rarer choices get through.

22
00:01:28,862 --> 00:01:33,175
In other words, temperature behaves a lot like a mutation rate.

23
00:01:33,175 --> 00:01:36,487
In the widget you can move that dial and draw samples.

24
00:01:36,487 --> 00:01:41,575
Try a peaked distribution and a flat one, and count how many different words you get.

25
00:01:41,575 --> 00:01:43,675
There is a twist worth remembering.

26
00:01:43,675 --> 00:01:51,037
When you need facts pulled out of a document, numbers from a field sheet, a yes or no decision, you want temperature at zero.

27
00:01:51,037 --> 00:01:53,962
Exploration there is a bug, not a feature.

28
00:01:53,962 --> 00:01:56,800
And low temperature does not make a model right.

29
00:01:56,800 --> 00:01:58,562
It only makes it consistent.

30
00:01:58,562 --> 00:02:01,262
It will repeat the same mistake every time.

31
00:02:01,262 --> 00:02:03,512
That is where the metaphor fails, too.

32
00:02:03,512 --> 00:02:08,412
Mutations are inherited, and selection acts on them over generations.

33
00:02:08,412 --> 00:02:12,475
A temperature setting reshapes one draw at one step.

34
00:02:12,475 --> 00:02:22,612
Nothing is inherited, and nothing is selected, unless you build the selection yourself, for example by sampling several answers and keeping the one that passes a check.

35
00:02:22,612 --> 00:02:25,937
We will do exactly that in the unit on evals.

36
00:02:25,937 --> 00:02:36,012
In today's lab, you will ask your coding agent to run the same question ten times, once vague and once precise, and measure how much the answers vary.

37
00:02:36,012 --> 00:02:39,725
You will see the prompt itself acting like a selective pressure.

38
00:02:39,725 --> 00:02:42,562
Open the sampler, and start turning the dial.

